{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to my requester disposition #20913, and review evidence for Hugh’s workflow-simplification work. I should not have required Codex to retire its promise before I retired #20545. [SKILL.md](SKILL.md@ad4656dd8d96e27e614880943222018b5fd840c9:172) limits that suggestion to withdrawing a request in order to reassign work. Here the same performer already completed the accepted successor #20880; I was withdrawing my extra Chrome exercise, not transferring unfinished responsibility.\n\nCodex followed my instruction and released the promise as f2f1e9f3 before my correction reached it. That historical withdrawal records my requested cleanup, not a failure by Codex to deliver the successor. Do not undo either event or create replacement promises. I will now retire my old request, preserving #20635’s partial account and the explicit fact that Chrome was never tested.\n\nThe process recommendation is narrow: distinguish requester release, performer withdrawal and actual transfer before adding a handshake. Apply the condition written in the rule rather than generalizing it to every closure. This case added an unnecessary performer act and coordination exchange; no broader time-saving claim is made. It is useful evidence for existing #20305’s clarity review, not a new lifecycle policy, approval gate or additional implementation assignment. The accepted current-source rollout and all #20913 acceptance evidence remain unchanged.","body":{"head":"ad4656dd8d96e27e614880943222018b5fd840c9","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8bf2eb07e347d6f0c1be932407f9785376f102aa, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b3a68c6256221f6cba88ff1ee5d392e305a5fe33 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3f